From: Angel Martinez <110217098+anmartinez1256@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 08:43:59 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 09/40] Metal diamond engine: UPML on the GPU, extension data
 in three buffers (snapshot cg21)

The UPML extension runs per tile on the GPU: Pre before the update (after the Mur
Pre stage, as the CPU calls the Pre hooks in reverse extension order), Post after it
(before the Mur Post stage), for the voltages and the currents, over the box cells in
the tile's ranges. The arithmetic follows clang's contraction of the CPU expressions
(vv*E - vvfo*flux = fma(vv, E, -(vvfo*flux)), flux + vvfn*E one fmadd). The
coefficients are a table of the distinct sets of all boxes with one index per
component and cell; the flux lives on the GPU. Overlapping boxes (not made by
Create_UPML) fall back to the CPU loops.

Metal has 31 buffer slots and the kernel used 29, so the extension data now come in
three buffers (read-only words, read-only floats, state) with an offset table.

The default threads per tile follow the tile size (the power of two at or below 3/5 of
a mountain's f4vectors per half-step, 256 to 1024; a K/threads sweep on the five
models that now run on the GPU). OPENEMS_DIAMOND_TILE also takes M:V (bench.py
splits its environment settings on commas).

The notch tutorial, notch x3 and the SMA launch now run on the GPU, port probes and
S-parameters bit-identical to the multithreaded engine with OPENEMS_FTZ=1;
diamond_check.sh 36 of 36 (new: PML_8 on six faces, PML mixed with Mur faces that
cross the PML boxes, with lumped elements).

